{Time limit 30 moves in 1hr 15' then 6 moves per 15'} 1. c4 Nf6 2. d4 g6 3. Nc3
d5 4. Bf4 Bg7 5. e3 O-O 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. Nxd5 Qxd5 8. Bxc7 Na6 {Forcing White
to return the Pawn.} 9. Bxa6 ({Not} 9. Bg3 $2 Bf5 10. a3 Rac8 11. Nf3 Rc2 $17 {
[Alatortsev - Lilienthal, Moscow 1936]}) 9... Qxg2 ({Pachman recommends} 9...
bxa6) 10. Qf3 Qxf3 11. Nxf3 bxa6 12. O-O {[9']} Bb7 {[14']} 13. Nd2 ({Both
Evans-Gligoric [Amsterdam 1964] Furman-Dubinin [Leningrad 1947] preferred} 13.
Ne5 {. Ian had also expected this so maybe I had unconsciously disrupted his
opening preparation.}) 13... Rac8 14. Rac1 Rfe8 {Presumably to force 15.....e5
but surprisingly this move prevents it!} 15. Rc5 {Now 15.....e5 isn't playable,
but without his King Rook on e8 it would have been!} Bf8 16. Rc3 e6 (16... e5 {
was the alternative that I had spent most time on.}) 17. a3 Be7 18. Rfc1 h5 {
[55']} ({Possibly} 18... Bd5 {could be considered.}) 19. Nb3 {[35']} Bd5 20.
Na5 {I was in two minds over this. The Knight may get out of play.} g5 {
Active counterplay} 21. h3 Kh7 22. Be5 Rxc3 23. Rxc3 Bd8 24. Nc6 Bb6 25. Nb8
Bb7 26. Nd7 Rc8 {[1hr 10']} 27. Nc5 Ba8 28. Nxa6 Rb8 29. Nc7 Bf3 30. Nb5 h4 31.
Nd6 Kg6 32. Nc8 ({Maybe this 9th consecutive move with the Knight is not as
good as} 32. e4) 32... Kh5 {Adjourned. Black has nasty threats down the h & g
files. Black's King Bishop is a poorer piece than mu Knightand I need to
actively oppose his KS intentions. I therefore sealed} 33. Ne7 {The game
resumed on 22/12/76. The best sealed move.} Re8 34. Bd6 Bd8 {As good as
anything.} ({Lines like} 34... f5 35. Rc8 Rxc8 36. Nxc8 g4 37. Kh2 gxh3 38.
Kxh3 Bg4+ 39. Kh2 h3 40. Bb8 {hold no hope for Black.}) 35. Nc6 Rg8 36. Ne5 Bd5
37. Rc8 (37. Nxf7 {is too dangerous.}) 37... f6 38. Ng4 {I preferred this to
38 Nf7 as it gives Black no counterchances.} (38. Nf7) 38... e5 {A pawn has to
go.} 39. dxe5 f5 40. Nf6+ {I gave this little thought, expecting to win easily
with two extra Pawns.} (40. Rxd8 fxg4 41. hxg4+ Kxg4 42. Rxg8 Bxg8 {is rather
similar to the game and Black looks to have a draw. It seems White may have
gone wrong earlier, (move 37?)}) 40... Bxf6 41. Rxg8 Bxg8 42. exf6 Kg6 43. Be5
Bd5 {I have great doubts whether this is winnable. The weak h-Pawn and Black's
hold on the white squares make life rather difficult for White.} 44. b4 a6 45.
a4 $2 {After this there's no hope. Whether it can be won by giving up the
h-Pawn is doubtful but I was playing too quickly in any event.} Bc6 46. a5 Kf7
47. Bc3 Bf3 48. Be5 Ke6 49. Kf1 Bd5 50. Bc3 Bf3 51. Bd4 Kf7 52. b5 {A forlorn
hope.} axb5 53. a6 Ke6 54. a7 b4 55. Kg1 g4 56. hxg4 fxg4 57. Kf1 b3 58. Kg1
Kf7 59. Kh2 Ke6 60. Bb2 Kf7 61. Be5 Ke6 62. e4 Bxe4 {Rather disappointing.}
1/2-1/2

